1
00:00:02,423 --> 00:00:02,551
Now,

2
00:00:03,081 --> 00:00:06,339
let's start with the left side here, with

3
00:00:06,611 --> 00:00:09,388
calling a function and with passing values

4
00:00:09,484 --> 00:00:10,993
for function parameters.

5
00:00:11,811 --> 00:00:12,164
And here,

6
00:00:12,774 --> 00:00:16,048
for writing clean code and for being able to

7
00:00:16,064 --> 00:00:19,113
call functions in a clean and understandable

8
00:00:19,113 --> 00:00:19,241
way,

9
00:00:19,851 --> 00:00:20,926
there is one simple rule.

10
00:00:21,616 --> 00:00:25,035
You should minimize the number of parameters

11
00:00:25,291 --> 00:00:26,335
your functions use.

12
00:00:27,313 --> 00:00:30,090
Because the more parameters a function uses,

13
00:00:30,282 --> 00:00:33,684
the harder it gets to call them. And harder

14
00:00:33,877 --> 00:00:35,915
here really means that it's harder to

15
00:00:35,915 --> 00:00:36,589
memorize

16
00:00:37,167 --> 00:00:39,237
which value should be passed as which

17
00:00:39,413 --> 00:00:41,772
argument. So the order of arguments can be

18
00:00:41,772 --> 00:00:44,404
confusing. And if you have to pass in a lot

19
00:00:44,404 --> 00:00:46,618
of arguments, it also simply is a lot of

20
00:00:46,634 --> 00:00:48,689
code to write. And ultimately that all

21
00:00:49,282 --> 00:00:51,978
leads to more code you have to read, more

22
00:00:52,107 --> 00:00:53,519
things you have to keep in mind.

23
00:00:54,433 --> 00:00:57,573
We can probably all agree that calling a

24
00:00:57,702 --> 00:01:00,826
function or method with no parameters is

25
00:01:00,826 --> 00:01:04,336
very simple. If we, for example, have a user

26
00:01:04,432 --> 00:01:07,172
object and on that user object we have a

27
00:01:07,172 --> 00:01:10,249
save method, then this is very easy to call

28
00:01:10,377 --> 00:01:12,669
and to read. We don't have to pass in

29
00:01:12,781 --> 00:01:15,601
multiple values and remember the order of

30
00:01:15,729 --> 00:01:18,037
arguments. We don't have to do anything of

31
00:01:18,133 --> 00:01:20,346
that. We have one function function call

32
00:01:20,715 --> 00:01:23,654
with no parameters. This therefore is very

33
00:01:23,702 --> 00:01:24,537
easy to understand.

34
00:01:25,790 --> 00:01:28,392
So it's easy to understand and easy to call.

35
00:01:28,745 --> 00:01:31,444
And therefore, this is the best possible way

36
00:01:31,540 --> 00:01:34,013
of calling a function, though of course I

37
00:01:34,126 --> 00:01:36,615
will say right away, this is not always the

38
00:01:36,680 --> 00:01:39,346
way you can do it. There are of course

39
00:01:39,346 --> 00:01:41,594
functions or methods which do require

40
00:01:41,594 --> 00:01:44,292
parameters. You can't refactor every

41
00:01:44,325 --> 00:01:46,541
function to take no parameters. That will

42
00:01:46,637 --> 00:01:47,825
just not be an option.

43
00:01:48,741 --> 00:01:51,213
Nonetheless, sometimes you can achieve this,

44
00:01:51,470 --> 00:01:53,686
and if you can, this therefore is the best

45
00:01:53,718 --> 00:01:55,982
possible option when it comes to

46
00:01:56,512 --> 00:01:59,354
ease of calling and to understanding this

47
00:01:59,418 --> 00:01:59,787
code.

48
00:02:00,799 --> 00:02:02,902
Still very good is if you need one

49
00:02:03,336 --> 00:02:06,852
parameter, one argument. For example, if you

50
00:02:06,868 --> 00:02:09,822
had a log function which takes the message

51
00:02:10,015 --> 00:02:11,829
as the one argument it wants.

52
00:02:12,825 --> 00:02:15,603
This is still very easy to understand and

53
00:02:15,635 --> 00:02:18,332
still very easy to call. You have to pass in

54
00:02:18,509 --> 00:02:21,062
one value. There is no order which you have

55
00:02:21,094 --> 00:02:23,824
to memorize. And if you read this code, it's

56
00:02:23,824 --> 00:02:26,826
crystal clear what it does. So this is still

57
00:02:26,955 --> 00:02:28,689
a very good option you can use.

58
00:02:29,989 --> 00:02:32,655
Now it already becomes a bit more tricky if

59
00:02:32,655 --> 00:02:36,043
you have 2 arguments, 2 parameters which

60
00:02:36,123 --> 00:02:37,118
need to be passed in.

61
00:02:37,856 --> 00:02:41,046
For example, if we have a Point object which

62
00:02:41,158 --> 00:02:43,114
we can instantiate by calling its

63
00:02:43,162 --> 00:02:45,583
constructor, which this code could do,

64
00:02:46,432 --> 00:02:49,815
where we then pass in 2 arguments, let's say

65
00:02:49,815 --> 00:02:53,197
the x and y coordinate of that point.

66
00:02:54,464 --> 00:02:57,477
This is still okay to understand because it

67
00:02:57,894 --> 00:03:01,453
is kind of common sense that probably the x

68
00:03:01,517 --> 00:03:04,402
value is the first argument and the y value

69
00:03:04,402 --> 00:03:06,855
is the second argument, but still there is

70
00:03:07,063 --> 00:03:09,115
some ordering which you need to keep in

71
00:03:09,195 --> 00:03:11,846
mind. The order could be switched and

72
00:03:11,926 --> 00:03:14,143
therefore this is acceptable to call,

73
00:03:14,738 --> 00:03:16,859
but you might have to think about it for a

74
00:03:16,859 --> 00:03:19,975
