77
00:03:25,277 --> 00:03:27,960
So therefore you should use this approach or

78
00:03:28,330 --> 00:03:30,563
write functions with more than one argument

79
00:03:31,061 --> 00:03:34,435
with caution. In some cases like here for a

80
00:03:34,435 --> 00:03:38,014
point, it is still absolutely fine because

81
00:03:38,062 --> 00:03:40,355
it is quite obvious that the first value

82
00:03:40,435 --> 00:03:42,985
will be the x value and the second value

83
00:03:43,017 --> 00:03:45,454
will be the y value. But for other

84
00:03:45,502 --> 00:03:48,437
functions, this might not be as clear, and

85
00:03:48,469 --> 00:03:50,746
then it immediately becomes harder to call

86
00:03:50,858 --> 00:03:52,943
them because you have to think about the

87
00:03:53,183 --> 00:03:56,134
order of arguments. And reading that code

88
00:03:56,470 --> 00:03:58,988
therefore also could become harder because

89
00:03:58,988 --> 00:04:01,906
it's not immediately obvious which argument

90
00:04:01,922 --> 00:04:03,189
